sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch16-03> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Where stiffness against bending in all directions is required, organisms use a hollow cylindrical construction with little or no material in the core, as in the thin-walled, almost empty wing bones of long-winged birds such as the albatross, quills, and hollow stems of reeds and straw.";
    dc:type "mathematical-law";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Structures that must resist bending are built as hollow tubes";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "D’Arcy Wentworth Thompson, On Growth and Form (1st ed., Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1917), Chapter XVI, “On Form and Mechanical Efficiency”; topic “Tubular construction of bones and stems”. Scope: long bones of vertebrates (especially bird wing bones), feather quills and cylindrical plant stems"
}
